</node>
<node id="&quot;ONUS OF PROOF THEREOF&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"SOCIAL SCIENCES"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The onus of proof refers to the burden placed on the employer to demonstrate that a given employment contract does not constitute a continuous contract, highlighting legal and social science aspects related to dispute resolution in labor relations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-2ebf8163b69d6052462eecc2153a2260</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 173}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CONTINUOUS CONTRACT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Continuous Contract is a term used in employment law to describe an ongoing contract, which may be determined based on the employee's work hours and previous employment period."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Continuous Contract is mentioned as an agreement under which an employee works continuously for at least 24 months before being laid off or put on leave without pay, indicating a long-term employment relationship."&lt;SEP&gt;"A continuous contract is an employment agreement that does not have a fixed end date and ensures the employee receives at least one rest day every seven days according to labor laws."&lt;SEP&gt;"A continuous contract refers to the type of employment agreement that must be in place for at least 40 weeks prior to qualify for paternity leave and related payments, as per legal requirements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Continuous Contract indicates the employment status of female employees, implying uninterrupted service before taking maternity leave."&lt;SEP&gt;"Continuous Contract refers to a contract of employment under which an employee is deemed by virtue of the provisions of the First Schedule to be in continuous employment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Continuous Contract refers to a type of employment agreement where an employee has been employed for at least 24 months, which provides certain rights and protections."&lt;SEP&gt;"Continuous Contract refers to a type of employment agreement where an employee has been in work for at least 12 months without interruption, which qualifies them for annual leave benefits."&lt;SEP&gt;"Continuous Contract refers to a type of employment contract that is ongoing and may have specific conditions related to retirement age, contributions, and severance benefits."&lt;SEP&gt;"Continuous Contract refers to an employment agreement that must be deemed monthly renewable unless otherwise agreed upon in writing."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e976980f19e9ab89febaf65027fa503e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9809ad52a0fec55dacd101bbfec86fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0aed3ae0b98488b1b913e6b49db19fee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e804bc54b158b7b2826228aa81b5372e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea2f0f182df099225a628487d487158f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7ea07c8002a487f595524992c8604b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c54e7deea495212bab465e4af100b7ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8d3158e721c74f203da21b92ec49085&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d9929bbede2eb72d013426b56ad4e6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da3daa00f8a571e7a58b273ea6df48ea</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 97}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 505}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ONUS OF PROOF&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The onus of proof for determining whether a contract of employment is a continuous contract lies with the employer if there is any dispute."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e976980f19e9ab89febaf65027fa503e</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 97}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 505}]</data>
</node>
