<node id="&quot;OCCUPATIONAL SAFETY OFFICER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">An Occupational Safety Officer (OSO) is a public official designated by the Commissioner, typically associated with organizations like the Labor Bureau. The OSO has extensive powers and responsibilities concerning workplace safety inspections and compliance with relevant regulations. These duties include entering premises without a warrant if reasonable grounds are present or obtaining magistrate's authorization based on sworn information. They can also request samples to be divided and take away records or statements under certain conditions, perform tests and examinations, seize evidence, ensure the proper handling of these samples for analysis, and require responsible persons to exhibit notices in workplaces regarding various aspects such as operational ordinances, plant installations, substance installations, and commercial activities.

The OSO is tasked with ensuring that safety standards are met both before and after workplace accidents or dangerous occurrences. They may conduct informal inquiries into such incidents and report findings in writing. Additionally, they receive notifications of workplace incidents according to legal requirements and assist public officers in exercising their functions related to occupational health and safety regulations. Intimidating an OSO is considered a criminal offense.

The role of the Occupational Safety Officer is defined under sections 20, 21(3)(a), and section 4(2A) of the Factories and Industrial Undertakings Ordinance (Cap. 59). Their primary responsibility involves ensuring compliance with occupational safety standards through inspections, inquiries, and other related functions.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-46d0d7db59a8dee1e09d7d4424293375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cd5c7a6a9ef486d89da782949b23d31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d781ae6a3974dcd155b4f5b736d6161&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd461abafa09b711df723a9e8d6a7aab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce083c97323a19a9162d045f2002c1d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbbad1d728178af8a023898a237d258a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea83763b873f6b32742c132cbac8d568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3282c20ec28d4bb14aec7fe4e4eafbe8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe4c625e0d88b6c7ccc901d0d7b85932&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20f049d1d30f5c16c93c8bc30a7dab75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b491d14ac4486a01edd1dfafab97ca07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e245666877c34f0ac196d032b544a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e25506f50e901e7cebe325e56cdc3f67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7881ceecf94346279b299fee8d79193&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da2837c3b5e351b2e1c63635e1c0a663&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3c3394a6b72977677a8a437b4151b9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58cf55b6baed9ac9a3c4052538486aa3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bca658a6c295daa0f1b015fffbbdfb79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05288c51fb6c9855d3f031e51b7e2be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4c1ae5b0a8aff1b3116e95deba9e3ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d2094275e74c7538edf07aca95a54a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7f5c7774940469ec261850f6dbd6b83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19f640b139895da0133f304114aeb8fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed932e3526aee6a4abf0328b0cfd58ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc560479538470069d89f317f702e111</data>
