<node id="&quot;SPECIAL ASSESSMENT BOARD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">The Special Assessment Board is an organization responsible for assessing and issuing certificates related to claims involving loss of earning capacity due to injuries or periods of absence from duty. This body operates under specific sections and subsections of a legal document, such as section 16E(8) or (9), and can handle assessments referred by the Ordinary Assessment Board when certain conditions are met, particularly those involving permanent partial incapacity resulting from unspecified injuries.

The Special Assessment Board may also be involved in reviewing objections from employers or employees and issuing relevant certificates. It has the authority to issue certificates under sections 16F and 16G(3), which affect employer-employee interactions related to incapacity and compensation claims. Additionally, decisions made by this board can be appealed to the District Court for further review.

The Special Assessment Board is a specialized body that provides a more detailed evaluation process compared to the Ordinary Assessment Board, focusing on specific sections such as 9(1A) and operating under conditions outlined in section 16E of the relevant regulations.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-514e0a6ffa9d391d835f484bdbaa5724&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7788fe09e12252d0bb714e4f775b4322&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21097621f6720ea0958fecf07d8dcf43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb828f2a7eae180f120101c1cd23e327&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f281684938925901a68a287ea35fbc44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0aa6a1ecbb96f241ec693e9b7796d393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779a6ebdcf1cc3fbd67a4dc22862c59e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3ba6e4c8e42988a064ffe8e2ff8f96e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a21e7be563e120a46acd1d6f45ede27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb0545d506978169e320db3a0cda4273&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da37f195f3ad2a893b50923a13911e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-038abcb26f772c8f77b5ead3c3b7177c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-924ba54f67533a1228efb89110dcadb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bad177b227e380cc066abb25e6c2cf7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f5ab2145d9ab1c03798719541af19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1eeedbfd93d66d01580f5f057b6a2dfb</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 140}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 619}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 1154}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;處長&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">The "處長" (Chief Officer) is a key official role within an organization or department, responsible for making critical decisions and issuing certificates. This position holds significant authority in determining legal responsibilities, assessing compensation according to regulations, and issuing certificates regarding medical fees. The Chief Officer has the power to give written consent for prosecution, make decisions regarding hearings and prosecutions, and approve certain actions under specific ordinances.

The "處長" is also involved in dispute resolution, making determinations on disputes and compensation assessments. They are typically responsible for deciding whether employees have the right to receive long-term service benefits based on their work-related injuries or illnesses. This role often involves consulting medical experts before issuing decisions.

In addition, the Chief Officer plays a crucial part in handling notifications related to workplace accidents, investigating employee deaths, and reporting on loss of working capacity according to legal requirements. They are authorized to conduct necessary inquiries and inform relevant family members about such matters.

The "處長" is also tasked with approving agreements between employees and employers, issuing temporary stop-work notices, and overseeing their implementation. This role involves taking appropriate actions in response to employee requests and making decisions that ensure compliance with regulations.

Overall, the "處長" holds a pivotal position within an organization or department, exercising significant authority over various aspects of decision-making, dispute resolution, compensation assessments, and legal notifications related to employees' rights and well-being.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-92d63fd21b0cce3b9e5c075238ab83af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad03aa80581d9d78c4971adade589820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb9dffd6c5cc9c95fa591e60b3b6694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c031adb2191e2fdd70a1cfb2171a003&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eae344452d3fef20d9a84a14e26b325a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b965639f18b2c29c5f672b6067f9d701&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d9a950f8e72e839cb27b535da95ff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56cceac4667a58d36b3976e9f95e0382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd5d9d61caba55ae3f9dfd2940ae47cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a95471afd1aaf3812ba594d6c8b0d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-193cb7d25870869b93d954860d0aaace&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d685de840aec3e84c6ab3d5c85a2c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-688810e0abd091776de88edcaaba1bb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0ab6c8718eab2752969763d7d57fe2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42e445b0642be8d3a51c65d52cad0740&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9abcb1aa4ccb5d467cb0175ca3011bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0b18fbc4380b6448b0da3a5d111b4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4dd58ad4c9957f63fe03ad87123a328&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50672759d0eedd36be50c0f02eae5b67</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 1}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 72}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 466}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"SOCIAL SCIENCES"</data>
  <data key="d1">The concept of years of service, MPFS (Medical and Pharmaceutical Fund Scheme) benefits, ORS (Occupational Retirement Schemes) plans, extended holidays, gratuities, labor relations, lump-sum payments, mandatory benefits, voluntary benefits, and the distinction between pre- and post-transition portions are all topics within the social science field of labor economics. Labor economics examines financial aspects of work and employee compensation over time, including various forms of compensation such as benefits, severance packages, and gratuities.

These discussions also intersect with human resource management in examining how work experience impacts compensation and benefits. The term "aggregate amount" in Section 1B suggests that mathematical calculations are involved in determining reductions or payments related to severance and gratuities.

Additionally, the document deals with labor relations and employee benefits, areas within social sciences that study human behavior in organizational settings. Labor relations involve interactions between employers and employees concerning their rights and responsibilities.

The term "aggregate amount" also implies a focus on quantitative analysis of financial aspects of work and employee compensation over time. The discussions around these topics are relevant to various industries such as government agencies, enterprises, and other organizations that must comply with national laws and regulations regarding holidays and benefits.

Furthermore, the concept of extended holidays can include considerations for overtime pay during statutory holidays, which may be addressed by software or services designed to calculate overtime compensation. These systems also manage sales related to commemorative days, reflecting how social sciences like sociology and anthropology study the impact and cultural significance of these events on people's lives.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-6dc88cb5a70d0e4c2d56e0663d5e01f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622dec92f9d3c73f201fbdc4fab3be5f</data>
</node>
